ing in data centers\n. That total spans his campaign, leadership PAC, joint fundraising committees and a pro-Paxton super PAC. It was \nagainst a war chest of $9.3 million as of June 30\n, so it is roughly 5% of his haul. \"Bankroll\" overstates that. The donor list also covers firms such as engineering and building-materials companies. The sources I found do not tie the figure to the Houston-area or Caldwell County developers named in the context (Montera, McCord, Tract, Edged).\n- Tax breaks: The state sales-tax exemption for qualifying data centers is real. \nQualifying data centers can avoid the 6.25% state sales tax on equipment and on electricity\n. \nThe Senate Finance Committee projects it will cost about $3.3 billion in 2028–29\n. The break comes from \na 2013 law\n, not from Paxton, and it now faces bipartisan pushback. Republican Sen. Bob Hall argued that \nit ultimately shifts costs to other taxpayers\n. The industry counters that \nfocusing on forgone revenue overl", "verdict_sources": ["https://www.notus.org/technology/ken-paxton-data-centers-texas-senate-race-james-talarico", "https://www.sacurrent.com/news/ken-paxtons-u-s-senate-campaign-has-pulled-in-nearly-450000-in-data-center-donations/", "https://www.tpr.org/economy-and-labor/2026-07-28/texas-lawmakers-question-billions-in-tax-breaks-for-data-centers", "https://communityimpact.com/central-austin/texas-legislature/texas-spends-billions-on-tax-breaks-for-data-centers-theres-a-bipartisan-legislative-push-to-repeal-them/", "https://www.datacenterknowledge.com/regulations/texas-pushes-ai-data-centers-to-pay-their-own-grid-costs", "https://www.tpr.org/government-politics/2026-08-11/data-center-industry-contributes-thousands-to-paxton-campaign", "https://www.houstonpublicmedia.org/articles/news/politics/election-2026/2026/08/11/559188/data-center-industry-contributes-thousands-to-ken-paxton-campaign-texas-attorney-general-james-talarico/"], "posts": 1, "views": 438052, "accounts": 1, "copies": 0, "push